{"summary":"The bill would expand eligibility for an individualized education account (IEA) to include a Tennessee resident child whose medical condition, as determined by the State Board of Education, prevents the child from attending school in person, if the child is entitled to attend public school. The existing disability-based eligibility route would require an active IEP listing one of 11 specified disabilities as the primary or secondary disability when the department receives the participation request, plus at least one of these conditions: the student attended a Tennessee public school for the full school year immediately before receiving an IEA, is enrolling in a Tennessee school for the first time, or received an IEA in the previous school year.

For a student eligible because of a medical condition, a participating school could be a home school as defined in the cited law. The bill would make Section 49-10-1404 inapplicable to those students and their home schools, and would specify that references to participating schools in two other provisions apply to qualifying nonpublic schools, not home schools.

A parent of a medically eligible student would have to use IEA funds only for expenses approved under Section 49-10-1403(b). The department would have to regularly monitor those funds for compliance. It could suspend or terminate the student's participation if the parent failed to meet the spending requirement; a student whose participation is terminated under this provision could not participate in the program again. The bill would take effect July 1, 2026."}
